<Project>

  <!--
    Shared .NET analysis baseline for the Stardew integration.

    Every csproj under integrations/stardew/ already sets
    TreatWarningsAsErrors=true, so this file deliberately does NOT set it:
    that priority belongs to each project. What this file fixes is the
    drift in *which analyzer level* each project uses - three of the ten
    csproj set AnalysisLevel themselves and seven did not, which means a
    newly added project silently lands outside analysis coverage.

    These two properties are the official .NET SDK mechanism for keeping
    analyzer configuration consistent across a solution without repeating
    it in every csproj.

    Not set here on purpose:
      - TreatWarningsAsErrors / CodeAnalysisTreatWarningsAsErrors:
        each project owns its warning policy; enabling it centrally would
        change seven projects at once. ONE exception to that priority is
        declared below (WarningsNotAsErrors, NU1900 and nothing else), because
        the code it demotes is about a check that could not run rather than
        about this tree's source.
      - Any third-party analyzer package (SonarAnalyzer, StyleCop,
        Meziantou, Roslynator): the SDK's built-in NetAnalyzers are not
        yet used to their full extent, and analyzer NuGet packages add
        restore time and version-conflict surface for no current benefit.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <AnalysisLevel>latest-recommended</AnalysisLevel>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    One language version for every project under integrations/stardew/, set here and nowhere
    else. Measured on 2026-11-16 before this line existed: not one of the eleven csproj named
    LangVersion, so all eleven compiled at whatever the SDK defaults for their target
    framework - C# 10 for net6.0 - and nothing in the tree said so. That default is invisible
    and moves with the SDK, which is the property this file replaces: a project added
    tomorrow inherits 12.0 instead of silently compiling at a different language version.

    WHY 12.0 AND NOT 13.0. The ceiling is the pinned compiler, not a preference: global.json
    pins SDK 8.0.424 (rollForward latestPatch -> 8.0.425), whose Roslyn accepts up to C# 12.
    Asking for 13.0 fails the build outright - `error CS1617: invalid option "13.0" for
    /langversion` - so a higher number here is not "more modern", it is a broken build. Raise
    this line only after global.json selects a newer SDK.

    WHY NO POLYSHARP. PolySharp exists to backfill the runtime types modern syntax needs on old
    target frameworks. Measured on net6.0 with TreatWarningsAsErrors (a throwaway net6.0 project
    at LangVersion 12.0): raw string literals, collection expressions over arrays, Span and
    List<T>, `ref readonly` parameters, `u8` literals and primary constructors all compile with
    0 warnings and 0 errors on the bare framework. Only inline arrays need a polyfilled
    `InlineArrayAttribute`, and this tree contains none (`grep InlineArray` over the whole
    Stardew integration: 0 hits). A package that backfills one unused feature is a dependency
    that buys nothing and is re-evaluated the day the first inline array is wanted.

    NOT SET HERE: TargetFramework. net6.0 is fixed by SMAPI - Stardew's mod host loads this
    assembly inside its own .NET 6 process - so it is an external constraint, not a choice this
    file may revisit. `tools/check-dotnet-target-and-language.mjs` asserts both halves.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <LangVersion>12.0</LangVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    NU1900 is "the vulnerability database could not be read", not "a vulnerability was found".
    NuGet raises it during restore when a package source is unreachable, and because every
    project here sets TreatWarningsAsErrors, NuGet routes its own warning through that and the
    restore fails - measured 2026-11-20: with https://api.nuget.org down,
    `dotnet build integrations/stardew/GameBuddy.Stardew.csproj -c Release` died with
    `error NU1900 ... 未能还原 ... 2 个错误` while every package it needs was already in the
    global cache (`dotnet restore` with an empty <packageSources> list succeeds in 88ms). The
    only thing the network was still needed for was the audit, and no pinned live run could
    build until it answered again.

    So exactly ONE code is demoted, and only on the way from warning to error: the audit still
    runs, and a vulnerability it FINDS (NU1901..NU1904) is still an error here. What is no longer
    an error is the diagnostic that says the check could not run at all.
    `tools/check-dotnet-target-and-language.mjs` asserts this list is exactly [NU1900] for every
    project in this tree that promotes warnings, so adding a second code to quiet a real finding
    reds a gate instead of shipping.

    WHAT THIS COSTS. Every project in this directory, including the Release output
    `host/scripts/build-production-artifact.mjs` stages into every generation: a machine that
    cannot reach its package source now publishes a generation whose package graph was NOT
    audited, and prints a warning saying so instead of stopping. That is intended - the one
    package here is build-time only (Pathoschild.Stardew.ModBuildConfig, PrivateAssets=all) and
    an unreachable diagnostic is not a defect in this tree - and the live-run harness records
    the fact as `NUGET-AUDIT:` in the pinned build's log rather than assuming the audit ran.

    It becomes unnecessary when restore can read a vulnerability database without reaching the
    internet, or when no project here promotes warnings.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <WarningsNotAsErrors>NU1900</WarningsNotAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    CS8524 IS NOT A DEFECT IN THIS TREE, AND CS8509 IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS.

    A `switch` expression over an enum that names every declared member is exhaustive over the set the product
    switches on, and Roslyn says so with two different diagnostics depending on whether it is: CS8509 names the
    MEMBER that has no arm (`The switch expression does not handle all possible values ... the pattern
    'Three.C' is not covered`), while CS8524 only says the same type could also hold an int that no member
    declared (`... which include unnamed enum values. For example, the pattern '(Three)3' is not covered`).

    Measured 2026-11-22 on a throwaway net6.0 project built with this file's properties: a switch covering all
    three members and no `_` produced CS8524 and nothing else; the same switch with `Three.C` removed produced
    CS8509 naming `Three.C`. So a catch-all arm is not what makes an enum switch safe - it is what stops the
    compiler from noticing the next member. Removing them (WorldModel's disposition projections, the wire tables
    in BridgeProtocol/BridgeSession, RefusalLayer, PostconditionReadPoint, BuildingContact) turns every one of
    those into a build failure at the projection that must gain a word.

    CS8524 is therefore not reported at all, and CS8509 still fails the build through TreatWarningsAsErrors in
    every project here. An unnamed enum value cannot reach these projections from the wire: each is reached
    either from the game's own reading (which yields a declared member) or from a parse that validates the word
    first, and the one place a cast-invalid value IS reachable - `BridgeSession.ToWire(BodyProgramNodeState)` -
    answers it before the switch rather than through a catch-all arm.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS8524</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
